simplybs: build the linux-gnu cross toolchains against glibc 2.31.

A shared library requires the newest glibc symbol version it binds to, and
that is decided by the headers and libc.so it was linked against -- the
toolchain's sysroot, not the user's machine. native/ct-tools had crosstool-NG
build GCC 15.2.0 against glibc 2.42, and the Sep 17 libraries came out needing
GLIBC_2.38:

    strlcpy strlcat                         2.38  (glibc's own copies now exist,
                                                   so nothing falls back)
    __isoc23_strtol ... __isoc23_sscanf     2.38  (g++ defines _GNU_SOURCE, and
                                                   2.38's headers redirect
                                                   strtol & co under it)
    arc4random arc4random_uniform           2.36
    pthread_*, dlopen & co                  2.34  (libpthread/libdl merge)

which is Ubuntu 24.04, Debian 13, Fedora 38 or newer. Ubuntu 22.04 cannot even
link against it. glibc 2.31 moves that to Ubuntu 20.04, Debian 11 and RHEL 9.
libc stays dynamically linked, so what runs is still the user's own patched
glibc; only the symbol versions the library asks for change.

The crosstool-NG configuration is not in this repository. It is
platforms/<tuple>/.config in MrCyjaneK/ct-ng-configs, pinned by commit, and
run.sh copies it into place and runs `ct-ng build` directly. So:

  - swap the glibc-2.42 blob for glibc-2.31. CT_FORBID_DOWNLOAD=y, so the
    tarball has to be listed here. sha256 9246fe44... is the one crosstool-NG
    1.28's own packages/glibc/2.31/chksum carries, and the file verified
    against its GNU signature (Siddhesh Poyarekar, BC7C7372637EC10C).

  - edit the platform .config before run.sh copies it: drop the glibc version
    choice and every symbol derived from it, select CT_GLIBC_V_2_31, and let
    `ct-ng olddefconfig` regenerate the rest. The derived symbols
    (CT_GLIBC_2_34_or_later and so on) are read by crosstool-NG's build
    scripts, and `ct-ng build` sources .config as-is without re-syncing it, so
    changing CT_GLIBC_VERSION alone would build 2.31 with 2.42's feature
    switches. The trailing grep fails the step if olddefconfig ever falls
    back to its default rather than accepting 2.31.

    Kernel headers stay at 4.20.17; glibc 2.31 needs 3.2 or newer.
    GCC stays at 15.2.0; crosstool-NG 1.28's kconfig offers 2.31 with it.

  - add make 4.3. glibc 2.31's Makefiles predate GNU make 4.4 and break
    under it, so for that version crosstool-NG's kconfig selects
    CT_GLIBC_DEP_MAKE_4_3 and builds make 4.3 as a companion tool. That
    only shows up once olddefconfig runs, and with CT_FORBID_DOWNLOAD the
    first attempt died at "make: download failed". sha256 e05fdde4... again
    matches crosstool-NG's packages/make/4.3/chksum and verified against
    its GNU signature (Paul D. Smith, B2508A90102F8AE3).

  - patch glibc 2.31 for simplybs' bison. It regenerates intl/plural.c from
    plural.y at build time with native/bison, which ct-tools depends on and
    simplybs pins to exactly 3.8, and that bison writes static prototypes for
    __gettextlex and __gettexterror that clash with plural.y's own:

        intl/plural.c:69:25: error: static declaration of '__gettextlex'
                             follows non-static declaration

    patches/ct-tools/glibc/2.31/ carries the upstream fix (glibc 2.34+),
    and the config step selects CT_PATCH_BUNDLED_LOCAL with
    CT_LOCAL_PATCH_DIR pointing there, after crosstool-NG's own patches.
    simplybs does not hash files under patches/, so a change to that patch
    alone will not rebuild the toolchain; bump the native/_ marker too.

    olddefconfig changes nothing else of substance: CT_VERSION reads
    UNKNOWN, CT_TARGET_VENDOR is dropped (run.sh sets
    CT_OMIT_TARGET_VENDOR anyway), and older version choices are listed.

  - add a linux-gnu env line to native/_. simplybs hashes a package from its
    own recipe plus its DIRECT dependencies' recipes, not their hashes. Every
    target library depends on native/_, not on native/ct-tools, so without
    this their hashes would not move, and the cached boost, openssl, unbound,
    zeromq and so on, compiled against 2.42's headers, would be reused and
    carry their 2.36/2.38 references straight into the new library. Scoped
    to *-linux-gnu so the Android, Apple and mingw caches are untouched.

The mingw toolchain rebuilds too, since the download list is not filtered by
host, but its configuration and inputs are unchanged.

Verified on x86_64-linux-gnu (toolchain, every dependency and the library
rebuilt from these recipes, with 0013): the newest glibc symbol is GLIBC_2.30,
NEEDED is libdl, libm, libpthread, libc and ld-linux, `ldd -r` resolves every
symbol on Ubuntu 20.04, Debian 11 and Ubuntu 22.04 where the Sep 17 library
failed on 64, 64 and 14, and the cxmr Go tests pass on Ubuntu 20.04's glibc
2.31 itself. aarch64-linux-gnu built the same way comes out identical in
all the static respects (GLIBC_2.30, same NEEDED, NOW, MONERO_* only), and
under qemu-user on Ubuntu 20.04 arm64 `ldd -r` resolves every symbol and the
cxmr Go tests pass. It has not been run on physical arm64 hardware.

